Zero trust removes location-based implicit trust. Every access crosses a policy boundary where the caller's identity, requested action/resource, context, and current policy are evaluated with least privilege. It is a strategy, not a product and not necessarily “mTLS plus JWT on every hop.”

- authenticate workload identity with mTLS/SPIFFE, cloud federation, or another short-lived credential appropriate to the platform;
- carry end-user/delegation context only when the downstream needs it, with narrow audience and scope;
- authorize at the service that owns the resource and invariant, not only at the gateway;
- segment network paths, minimize Kubernetes RBAC/service-account permissions, protect secrets/keys, and record decisions;
- continuously patch, inventory, and verify workloads and artifacts—identity does not make compromised code trustworthy.

:::deep
A mesh can automate transport mTLS and workload certificates, but application authorization still needs domain context. NetworkPolicy constrains paths but does not authenticate a business actor. A signed token proves claims only after issuer/audience/time/key validation and does not replace object ownership checks.

Design degraded behavior: what happens if the CA, policy engine, introspection endpoint, JWKS endpoint, or mesh control plane is unavailable? Cache only what is safe, bound staleness, make fail-open/fail-closed choices per operation risk, and rehearse root/key rotation.

For PII/payment systems, map controls to actual data flows and compliance scope. A mandatory technology checklist can increase blast radius; a small system may achieve the same principles with cloud workload identity, TLS, service-local authorization, and tight IAM without operating a mesh.
:::
